The Small Business Administration (rivervalleyloans) guarantees loans for small-business owners through its network of partner lenders. There are several SBA loan programs you might consider when you need working capital; the 7(a) program is one of the most popular. The 7(a) program doesn’t require collateral for loans of up to $50,000, which is helpful if you only need to borrow a smaller amount of money.

U.S. Small Business Administration. "Types of 7(a) Loans."

For loans $50,001 to $500,000, the SBA requires lenders to collateralize loans to the maximum extent possible, up to the loan amount. (Lenders must follow "all the same policies and procedures that it has established and implemented for its similarly-sized, non-SBA guaranteed commercial loans.") However, the SBA has a policy that not having adequate collateral is not a sufficient reason for a loan to be denied.
